(1) $S_{\triangle BDF} = (1-x)y zS_{\triangle ABC}$ and $S_{\triangle CEF} = x(1-y)(1-z)S_{\triangle ABC}$;

(2) $\sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle BDF}} + \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle CEF}} = \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle ABC}}$.

positive_text:
In triangle $\triangle PBC$, $\angle PBC = 60^\circ$. A tangent to the circumcircle $w$ of triangle $\triangle PBC$ at point $P$ intersects the line $CB$ at point $A$. Points $D$ and $E$ are on segment $PA$ and the circle $w$ respectively, such that $\angle DBE = 90^\circ$ and $PD = PE$. The lines $BE$ and $PC$ intersect at point $F$. It is known that lines $AF$, $BP$, and $CD$ meet at a common point.
(1) Prove that $BF$ bisects $\angle PBC$;
(2) Find the value of $\tan \angle PCB$.

negatives.text:
In $\triangle PBC$, $\angle PBC = 60^\circ$. The tangent at point $P$ to the circumcircle $w$ of $\triangle PBC$ intersects with line $CB$ at $A$. Points $D$ and $E$ lie on the line segment $PA$ and circle $w$ respectively, such that $\angle DBE = 90^\circ$ and $PD = PE$. $BE$ and $PC$ meet at $F$. It is given that lines $AF$, $BC$ and $CD$ are concurrent.
(1) Prove that $BF$ is the bisector of $\angle PBC$;
(2) Find the value of $\tan \angle PCB$.

positive_text:
In an acute triangle $\triangle ABC$, let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of arcs $\widehat{BC}$ and $\widehat{AC}$, respectively, on the circumcircle of the triangle. Draw a line through $C$ parallel to $MN$, intersecting the circumcircle again at point $P$. Let $I$ be the incenter of $\triangle ABC$, and extend $PI$ to meet the circle again at point $T$.

(1) Show that the product $MP \times MT$ is equal to $NP \times NT$;

(2) For any point $Q$ (not equal to $A, T, B$) on arc $\widehat{AB}$ not containing $C$, let $I_1$ and $I_2$ be the incenters of $\triangle AQC$ and $\triangle QCB$, respectively. Show that the points $Q$, $I_1$, $I_2$, and $T$ lie on the same circle.

negatives.text:
In an acute triangle $\triangle ABC$, let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of arcs $\widehat{BC}$ and $\widehat{AC}$, respectively, on the circumcircle of the triangle. Draw a line through $C$ perpendicular to $MN$, intersecting the circumcircle again at point $P$. Let $I$ be the incenter of $\triangle ABC$, and extend $PI$ to meet the circle again at point $T$.

(1) Show that the product $MP \times MT$ is equal to $NP \times NT$;

(2) For any point $Q$ (not equal to $A, T, B$) on arc $\widehat{AB}$ not containing $C$, let $I_1$ and $I_2$ be the incenters of $\triangle AQC$ and $\triangle QCB$, respectively. Show that the points $Q$, $I_1$, $I_2$, and $T$ lie on the same circle.

In an acute triangle $\triangle ABC$, let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of arcs $\widehat{BC}$ and $\widehat{AC}$, respectively, on the circumcircle of the triangle. Draw a line through $C$ parallel to $MN$, intersecting the circumcircle again at point $P$. Let $I$ be the centroid of $\triangle ABC$, and extend $PI$ to meet the circle again at point $T$.

(1) Show that the product $MP \times MT$ is equal to $NP \times NT$;

(2) For any point $Q$ (not equal to $A, T, B$) on arc $\widehat{AB}$ not containing $C$, let $I_1$ and $I_2$ be the incenters of $\triangle AQC$ and $\triangle QCB$, respectively. Show that the points $Q$, $I_1$, $I_2$, and $T$ lie on the same circle.

positive_text:
Let $m$ and $k$ be non-negative integers, and suppose $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ is a prime. Show that

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. The smallest positive integer $n$ such that $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$ is $2^{m+1} p^k$.

negatives.text:
Suppose that $m$ and $k$ are non-negative integers, and $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ is a prime number. Prove that

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^k}$;

b. $2^{m+1} p^k$ is the smallest positive integer $n$ satisfying the congruence equation $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^k}$.

Suppose that $m$ and $k$ are non-negative integers, and $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ is a prime number. Prove that

a. $2^{2^m} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. $2^m p^k$ is the smallest positive integer $n$ satisfying the congruence equation $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$.

positive_text:
\text{(1) Show: in the complex plane, for every line through the origin except the real axis, there is at most one complex number } z \text{ such that } \frac{1+z^{23}}{z^{64}} \text{ is a real number.}

\text{(2) Show: for any nonzero complex number } a \text{ and any real number } \theta, \text{ the equation}
}
\begin{equation*}
1 + z^{23} + a z^{64} = 0
\end{equation*}
\text{has at least one solution in the set}
}
\begin{equation*}
S_{\theta} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} \mid \mathrm{Re}(z \cdot e^{-i\theta}) \ge |z| \cdot \cos \frac{\pi}{20}\}.
\end{equation*}

