positive_text:
\text{An equation } P(x) = Q(y) \text{ is called } \textbf{interesting} \text{ if both } P \text{ and } Q \text{ are polynomials with integer coefficients, degree at least 1, and there are infinitely many natural number solutions to the equation. If there exists a polynomial } R \text{ with rational coefficients such that } F(x) = R(P(x)) \text{ and } G(y) = R(Q(y)), \text{ we say that } F(x) = G(y) \text{ } \textbf{results from} \text{ } P(x) = Q(y).

\text{a) Let } S \text{ be an infinite set of pairs of natural numbers. We say that } S \text{ } \textbf{satisfies} \text{ an interesting equation } P(x) = Q(y) \text{ if every pair in } S \text{ satisfies the equation. Prove that there exists an interesting equation } P_o(x) = Q_o(y) \text{ such that any interesting equation satisfied by } S \text{ is obtained from } P_o(x) = Q_o(y) \text{ through this result process.

\text{b) The } \textbf{degree} \text{ of an interesting equation is the highest degree of } P \text{ and } Q. \text{ An interesting equation is called } \textbf{primitive} \text{ if it cannot be obtained from any other interesting equation of lower degree. Show that if } P(x) = Q(y) \text{ is a primitive interesting equation and both } P \text{ and } Q \text{ are monadic polynomials, then the degrees of } P \text{ and } Q \text{ are coprime.}

