positive_text:
Find the biggest whole number $ k $ (with $ k \geq 2 $) such that there exists a whole number $ n $ (with $ n \geq k $) for which the following is always true: If you pick any group of $ n $ consecutive positive numbers, then you can always select $ k $ numbers from that group that meet these two conditions:
1. None of the selected numbers is divisible by $ 6 $, $ 7 $, or $ 8 $;
2. For any two selected numbers, the difference between them is not divisible by at least one of $ 6 $, $ 7 $, or $ 8 $.

negatives.text:
Find the largest integer $ k $ ($ k \geq 2 $), for which there exists an integer $ n $ ($ n \geq k $) such that from any collection of $ n $ consecutive positive integers one can always choose $ k $ numbers, which verify the following conditions:
1. each chosen number is divisible by at least one of $ 6 $, $ 7 $, or $ 8 $;
2. the positive difference of any two different chosen numbers is not divisible by at least one of the numbers $ 6 $, $ 7 $, or $ 8 $.

Find the largest integer $ k $ ($ k \geq 2 $), for which there exists an integer $ n $ ($ n \geq k $) such that from any collection of $ n $ consecutive positive integers one can always choose $ k $ numbers, which verify the following conditions:
1. each chosen number is not divisible by $ 6 $, $ 7 $, and $ 8 $;
2. the positive difference of any two different chosen numbers is divisible by at least one of the numbers $ 6 $, $ 7 $, or $ 8 $.

Find the largest integer $ k $ ($ k \geq 2 $), for which there exists an integer $ n $ ($ n \geq k $) such that from any collection of $ n $ consecutive positive integers one can always choose $ k $ numbers, which verify the following conditions:
1. each chosen number is not divisible by $ 6 $, $ 7 $, and $ 8 $;
2. the positive difference of any two different chosen numbers is not divisible by all of the numbers $ 6 $, $ 7 $, and $ 8 $.

positive_text:
There are three sequences of positive integers, each growing strictly: $A = a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots$, $B = b_1, b_2, b_3, \ldots$, and $C = c_1, c_2, c_3, \ldots$. Every positive integer is in exactly one of these three sequences. For every positive integer $n$, the following rules apply:
(1) $c_{a_n} = b_n + 1$;
(2) $a_{n+1} > b_n$;
(3) the number $c_{n+1} c_n - (n+1) c_{n+1} - n c_n$ is even.
Determine $a_{2010}$, $b_{2010}$, and $c_{2010}$.

