positive_text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ be a function such that $f(n) > n$ for all $n$. Prove that:
a) there is a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, that approaches $0$, with the property that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$ for all $n$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is another decreasing sequence of real numbers that approaches $0$, then there exists a sequence $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of positive real numbers that is decreasing and approaches $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$ for all $n$.

negatives.text:
