- Each $a_{i}$ is less than $2^{2019}$;
- For each $i$ from 1 to 2018, $b_{i} = a_{i-1} + a_{i}$ and $c_{i} = a_{i-1} \mid a_{i}$;
where $\mid$ is the bitwise or operation on the binary representations of the numbers?

positive_text:
Suppose $n > 1$ is an integer. You have a square grid with $n$ rows and $n$ columns. Each small square in the grid is either black or white, with the following rules:
- All black squares are connected: you can move from any black square to any other black square by moving only through black squares that share a side;
- All white squares are connected: you can move from any white square to any other white square by moving only through white squares that share a side;
- In any $2 \times 2$ block of squares, there must be at least one black and one white square.

What is the largest possible difference between the total number of black squares and the total number of white squares in the grid, in terms of $n$?

negatives.text:
Let $n > 1$ be a positive integer. Each unit square in an $n \times n$ grid of squares is colored either black or white, such that the following conditions hold:
- Any two black squares can be connected by a sequence of black squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any two white squares can be connected by a sequence of white squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any $2 \times 2$ subgrid contains at least one black square.

Determine, with proof, the maximum possible difference between the number of black squares and white squares in this grid (in terms of $n$).

